- If A is true, then B must be false (since A and B cannot both be true).\n   - If A is true, then C must be false (since A and C cannot both be true).\n   - If A is true, then D must be false (since A and D cannot both be true).\n   - If A is false, then B must be true (since A and B cannot both be false).\n   - If A is false, then C must be true (since A and C cannot both be false).\n   - If A is false, then D must be true (since A and D cannot both be false).\n\n5. **Identify the Only True Statement:**\n   - If A is true, then B, C, and D are false.\n   - If A is false, then B, C, and D are true, which contradicts the condition that only one statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the only consistent scenario is when A is true, and B, C, and D are false. Hence, the correct answer is:\n\n**B) A is true, but no one violated the rules of operation.**",
