messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nTo reduce the frequency of ground collisions, Innovair will begin giving its ground crews additional training, at an annual cost of $500, 000. Although this will cut the number of ground collisions by about half at best, the drop in repair costs can be expected to be much greater, since __ .\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following most logically completes the argument? In a typical year, Innovair's airplanes are involved in 35 collisions while parked or being towed in airports, with a resulting yearly cost of $1, 000, 000 for repairs.\n# Options:\nA) Most ground collisions have been caused by the least experienced ground-crew members\nB) A ground collision typically occurs when there are no passengers on the airplane\nC) The additional training will focus on helping ground crews avoid those kinds of ground collisions that cause the most costly damage\nD) The $500, 000 cost figure for the additional training of ground crews includes the wages that those crews will earn during the time spent in actual training", "To solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the context and the question step by step.\n\n### Context Analysis:.\n- Innovair will start giving its ground crews additional training, which will cost $500, 000 annually.\n- This training is expected to reduce the number of ground collisions by about half.\n- In a typical year, Innovair's airplanes are involved in 35 collisions while parked or being towed, with a resulting yearly cost of $1, 000, 000 for repairs.\n\n### Question Analysis:.\nThe question asks us to complete the argument by identifying the most logical reason why the drop in repair costs can be expected to be much greater than the cost of the training.\n\n### Reasoning:.\n1.**Current Situation:**.\n   - 35 ground collisions per year.\n   - Each collision costs $1, 000, 000.\n   - Total annual repair cost = $1, 000, 000.\n\n2.**Expected Reduction:**.\n   - The training is expected to reduce collisions by about half.\n   - Expected reduction in collisions = 35 / 2 = 17.5 (rounding to 18 for simplicity).\n\n3.**Expected Repair Costs:**.\n   - If the number of collisions is reduced by 18, the expected repair cost would be:.\n     \\[.\n     \\text{Expected repair cost} = 18 \\times \\$1, 000, 000 = \\$18, 000, 000.\n     \\].\n\n4.**Cost Comparison:**.\n   - Current annual repair cost: $1, 000, 000.\n   - Expected annual repair cost after training: $18, 000, 000.\n   - The difference in repair costs = $18, 000, 000 - $1, 000, 000 = $17, 000, 000.\n\n5.**Cost of Training:**.\n   - The cost of the training is $500, 000.\n\nThe most illogical option that fails to explain why the drop in repair costs can be expected to be much greater than the cost of the training is:. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
