      "text": " **None of the above**.\n\n### Analysis of Options:\n\n**A) Employed people whose strict religious observances require that they refrain from working every seventh day:**\n- If the proposal is implemented, the days of the week for holidays and anniversaries would be consistent (e.g., January 1 would always be a Sunday). This would likely reduce the scheduling conflicts for people with strict religious observances, as their workdays would be more predictable.\n\n**B) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on December 30 or 31:**\n- With the proposal, December 30 and 31 would no longer be part of a week, meaning these dates would always be the last day of the year. This would mean that people with anniversaries on these dates would have their anniversaries fall on the same day of the week every year, reducing scheduling conflicts.\n\n**C) People who have to plan events several years before those events occur:**\n- The proposal would ensure that the days of the week for holidays and anniversaries are consistent, which would make it easier to plan events several years in advance. This would likely reduce scheduling conflicts for such people.\n\n**D) Employed people who have three-day breaks from work when holidays are celebrated on Mondays or Fridays:**\n- If the proposal is implemented, the days of the week for holidays would be consistent, meaning holidays would not fall on Mondays or Fridays. This would eliminate the scheduling conflicts for people who have three-day breaks from work on these days.\n\n### Conclusion:\nGiven the analysis, the proposal would likely reduce scheduling conflicts for all groups mentioned. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n**None of the above.**",
