messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWe ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?\n# Options:\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\nC) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.\nD) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.", "To solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the context and the question step by step.\n\n### Context Analysis:.\nThe context states that we should focus only on the intrinsic properties of a work of art.The intrinsic properties are those that are directly presented in our experience of the artwork.The extrinsic properties (those not directly presented) are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with the artwork.\n\n### Question Analysis:.\nThe question asks which option, when added to the premises, makes the conclusion logically follow.The conclusion is that what is really aesthetically relevant is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n### Premises:.\n1.We ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art.\n2.The intrinsic properties are those that are directly presented in our experience of the artwork.\n3.What is really aesthetically relevant is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n### Conclusion:.\nThe conclusion is that what is aesthetically relevant is what is directly presented, not what it symbolizes.\n\n### Evaluating the Options:.\n\n**A) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.**.\n- This option introduces the idea that symbolic properties exist, but it does not directly address the relevance of intrinsic properties.It does not help in making the conclusion that what is directly presented is what is aesthetically relevant.\n\nThe option that, when added to the premises, makes the conclusion logically follow is option C.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
